{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q020_syntaxin_fragment_reclassification","answer":"Verified SCOPe facts (release 2.08, current stable; older release 1.75 queried for comparison). All syntaxin N-terminal domains from the three structures are in the same family a.47.2.1 (t-snare proteins), superfamily a.47.2, fold a.47 (STAT-like), class a. The fold a.47 is described as '4 long helices; bundle, left-handed twist (coiled coil); right-handed superhelix'. The fragment structures: d1br0a_ (sunid 17772) and d1ez3a_/d1ez3b_/d1ez3c_ (sunids 17768-17770) sit under the curated protein node 'Syntaxin 1A N-terminal domain' (sunid 47663) and carry the curator comment 'three-helical fragment; similar to one spectrin repeat'. In 2.08 each of these fragment domains additionally carries the machine-readable heterogeneity flag 'ms' ('missing some secondary structures that made up less than one-third of the common domain'); in 1.75 the same domains had only the free-text comment (d1br0a_ also 'mutant') and no ms flag. The full-domain structure 1dn1 was obsoleted by 3c98 on 2008-03-25. In 1.75, d1dn1b_ was a curated domain (sunid 17771) under the Syntaxin 1A N-terminal domain protein node with no caveats. In 2.08, d1dn1b_ is a new record (sunid 302344) under the protein node 'automated matches' (sunid 191190) with the comment 'automated match to d3c98b_'; the curated, unflagged complete domain is now d3c98b_ (sunid 173092, 3c98 chain B) under the Syntaxin 1A N-terminal domain protein node. No warnings or heterogeneity records exist at the family (47662) or superfamily (47661) nodes.

Interpretation. (1) The family and superfamily relationships among all five domains are unchanged and remain SCOPe's positive homology assertion: they are the same protein, so the fragment status does not weaken common ancestry. (2) What changes is the evidential basis of the fold assignment. A three-helix fragment cannot by itself exhibit the four-helix STAT-like topology that defines fold a.47; its fold placement is inherited from protein identity with the complete domain (1dn1 B / 3c98 B), not demonstrated by the fragment's own structure. SCOPe 2.08 now encodes this as an 'ms' heterogeneity record rather than only a comment, so fragment-based fold assignments should be read as inherited and incomplete, and any automated pipeline should consume this flag. (3) The curator note that the fragment resembles one spectrin repeat illustrates the danger: a three-helix bundle is a common architecture, and structure comparison on a partial domain could suggest the wrong fold. SCOPe asserts no homology between syntaxin and spectrin, and the spectrin lineage was not retrieved here, so no fold-level or evolutionary statement about spectrin should be drawn from the comment. (4) The reference for the complete domain has moved: 1dn1 chain B is now only an automated match to d3c98b_. Automated status is not low quality, but the curated full-length representative for template use, benchmark positives, or alignment seeding in 2.08 is d3c98b_, not the ms-flagged fragments and not the reissued 1dn1 record. Fragments should be excluded or stratified in structure-based datasets while remaining valid family members for sequence-based work. (5) Function transfer: shared family supports close ancestry but does not establish Munc18/nSec1 binding mode, the closed conformation, or other functional states; those require the structure papers or experiments, which were not retrieved.

Limits. The API shows that the ms flag was added between 1.75 and 2.08 but does not expose the curatorial rationale. Residue ranges, sequence lengths, and SPACI/quality records were not retrieved, so no ranking of 1br0, 1ez3, 1dn1, or 3c98 by quality is made. The 2.08-2026-08-16 periodic update was not queried; all facts are from stable 2.08 and 1.75.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info","mcp__scope__get_scop_pdb_entry","mcp__scope__get_scop_domains","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1br0","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1br0/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1ez3","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1ez3/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1dn1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1dn1/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/3c98","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/3c98/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1br0a_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1br0a_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1br0a_/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ez3a_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ez3a_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ez3a_/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ez3b_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ez3b_/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1dn1b_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1dn1b_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1dn1b_/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3c98b_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3c98b_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3c98b_/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47654/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47661/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47661/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47662/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47662/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47663/annotations","/api/v1/releases/1.75/pdb/1dn1/domains","/api/v1/releases/1.75/sids/d1dn1b_/parents","/api/v1/releases/1.75/sids/d1dn1b_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/1.75/sids/d1br0a_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/1.75/sids/d1br0a_/homology","/ap